th the traffic signal at Alder and 4th as a condition of approval.\n\nLet me start with traffic, because I live with it. Some mornings around eight, cars on Alder already back up past my house. So when I read that this project would add 310 car trips a day, I took it seriously. I also read the rest of the traffic study. Without changes, the intersection at Alder and 4th would drop from a C to a D at rush hour. But Cascade Housing Partners has offered to pay for a signal there, and the study says that with the signal, the intersection stays at a C. That's why I'm asking you to require it. If the signal is a condition of the permit, the traffic gets handled, and it isn't just a promise.\n\nOn parking, the plan has 72 spaces for 64 apartments. That's more than one space per home.\n\nNow here's why this matters. Your own county housing report says we're short about 2,100 affordable homes. Right now, 412 households are on the housing authority's waiting list, and the average wait is three years. Every one of these 64 apartments would rent to a household earning under 60 percent of the area median income. We're talking about working people, seniors, and families who are already waiting.\n\nSixty-four homes won't close a gap of 2,100. But we close that gap one project at a time, and it's hard to find a better site than this one. It's 1.8 acres of pavement that sits empty six days a week. Nobody is being displaced, and no open space is lost. The congregation that owns the land wants to do this.\n\nI've watched this neighborhood for almost twenty years. I'd rather see that lot full of neighbors than empty six days a week.\n\nPlease approve the permit, and make the traffic signal a condition. Thank you.\n</final_document>\n\nThis runs about 390 words, which comes to roughly 2:45 at a steady speaking pace. Read it aloud once with a timer before tonight.\n\n- **Speaking for yourself:** You didn't know whether you're officially representing the church, so the speech has you spea